---
const regimes = [
  {
    title: "Foundation Models",
    example: "e.g., LLMs",
    rules: "Complex, contextual language patterns",
    signals: "Rich text and training signals",
    solution: "Pretraining & scaling",
  },
  {
    focus: true,
    title: "Human Digital Markets",
    example: "My research focus",
    rules: "Adaptive decisions & strategic responses",
    signals: "Policy-shaped data; missing counterfactuals",
    solution: "?",
  },
  {
    title: "Closed Games",
    example: "e.g., AlphaGo Zero",
    rules: "Fixed rules and explicit rewards",
    signals: "Repeatable self-play simulations",
    solution: "Simulation, search & RL",
  },
  {
    title: "Classical Physics",
    example: "e.g., Planetary Orbits",
    rules: "Stable governing laws",
    signals: "Limited real-world observations",
    solution: "Theory-driven modeling",
  },
];

const transfers = [
  {
    source: "Foundation Models → Digital Markets",
    description: "Use high-dimensional learning, language reasoning, and multimodal representations to incorporate richer information into empirical models.",
  },
  {
    source: "Classical Physics → Digital Markets",
    description: "Borrow theory-guided modeling to represent how preferences, beliefs, and incentives generate observed decisions, rather than merely fitting behavioral patterns.",
  },
  {
    source: "Closed Games → Digital Markets",
    description: "Use experiments and causal inference to identify how behavior changes under alternative policies when real-world decisions cannot be replayed.",
  },
];
---

<section class="learning-matrix" aria-labelledby="matrix-title">
  <h2 class="section-title" id="matrix-title">
    The Limits of Scaling<br />
    <span class="matrix-subtitle">— An Empirical Learning Matrix</span>
  </h2>
  <p class="matrix-intro">
    In settings such as digital platforms and online privacy, why can we not replicate the success of scaling laws in natural language, code, and math? To make sense of this, I map out four modeling environments below, revealing where conventional approaches reach their limits and where new possibilities emerge.
  </p>

  <div class="matrix-layout">
    <div class="information-axis">
      <p class="axis-title">← Effective Information Availability</p>
      <div class="information-labels">
        <p><strong>High</strong><span>Rich Signals &amp; Feedback</span></p>
        <p><strong>Low</strong><span>Limited Signals &amp; Feedback</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="axis-title rules-axis-title">Complexity of Rules <span aria-hidden="true">↑</span></p>
    <div class="rules-axis">
      <p class="rules-label"><strong>High</strong><span>Complex &amp; Adaptive</span></p>
      <p class="rules-label"><strong>Low</strong><span>Simple &amp; Stable</span></p>
    </div>

    <div class="matrix-grid" role="group" aria-label="Four regimes of empirical modeling by complexity of rules and effective information availability">
      {regimes.map((regime) => (
        <article class:list={["regime", regime.focus && "matrix-focus"]}>
          <h3 class="entry-title">{regime.title}</h3>
          <p class="example">{regime.example}</p>
          <h4>Condition</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>{regime.rules}</li>
            <li>{regime.signals}</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="solution"><strong>Solution:</strong> {regime.solution}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="matrix-explanation">
    Empirical models work best in the lower-left corner, where rules are simple and stable, and information is abundant. Digital markets rarely offer either: people and firms keep adapting to each other, while available data are often limited and biased. More data and computation alone cannot overcome these fundamental limitations.
  </p>
  <p class="method-introduction">
    This motivates me to learn from three established modeling paradigms, adapting and combining their strengths to address the distinct challenges of digital markets:
  </p>

  <div class="method-transfers" aria-label="Ideas from established approaches applied to digital markets">
    {transfers.map((transfer) => (
      <article class="transfer">
        <h3 class="entry-title">{transfer.source}</h3>
        <p>{transfer.description}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="research-goal">
    My goal is to understand what observed behavior alone cannot reveal—preferences, incentives, and counterfactual responses—and build that understanding directly into the design of algorithms and market mechanisms.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .learning-matrix {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .matrix-subtitle {
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
  }

  .matrix-intro {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-5);
  }

  .matrix-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-areas: "rules-title information" "rules panels";
    gap: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
    max-width: 56rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    /* Offset the axis column so the 2x2 grid itself sits on the page centre. */
    padding-inline-end: calc(7.5rem + var(--space-4));
  }

  .information-axis,
  .rules-axis-title,
  .rules-axis {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .information-axis {
    grid-area: information;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .information-labels {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
  }

  .axis-title,
  .information-labels strong,
  .rules-label strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .information-labels strong,
  .rules-label strong {
    display: block;
  }

  .information-axis p,
  .rules-axis p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .rules-axis {
    grid-area: rules;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: repeat(2, 1fr);
    align-items: center;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .rules-axis-title {
    grid-area: rules-title;
    align-self: end;
    margin: 0;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .matrix-grid {
    grid-area: panels;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    grid-template-rows: repeat(2, 1fr);
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  }

  .regime {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-width: 0;
    padding: var(--space-5);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .regime:nth-child(odd) {
    border-inline-end: var(--border);
  }

  .regime:nth-child(-n + 2) {
    border-block-end: var(--border);
  }

  .matrix-focus {
    background: var(--color-matrix-focus);
  }

  .regime .entry-title,
  .transfer .entry-title {
    font-size: var(--text-body);
  }

  .example {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 var(--space-3);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
  }

  .regime h4 {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
  }

  .regime ul {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 var(--space-3);
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
  }

  .regime li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
  }

  .solution {
    margin: auto 0 0;
    padding-top: var(--space-2);
    border-top: var(--border);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .solution strong {
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .matrix-explanation {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
  }

  .method-introduction {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
  }

  .method-transfers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-4) var(--space-7);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  .transfer p {
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
  }

  .research-goal {
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 47.5rem) {
    .matrix-layout {
      padding-inline-end: 0;
    }

    .method-transfers {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    /* Keep the rules axis beside the rows it labels; only its High/Low words fit at this width. */
    .matrix-layout {
      grid-template-columns: 2.25rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      grid-template-areas: "rules-title rules-title" ". information" "rules panels";
      column-gap: var(--space-2);
    }

    .rules-axis-title {
      text-align: left;
    }

    .rules-label span {
      display: none;
    }

    .regime {
      padding: var(--space-3);
      font-size: var(--text-small);
    }
  }
</style>
